course: dictionary
lesson: 10100
title: Thimble: one word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around them, tells you which meaning the writer wants. Ask yourself what the thing is or what is happening. «Most needle pricks come from pushing the needle towards your own fingers; a thimble protects the pushing finger.» Here a thimble is something you wear, because it protects a finger.
explain: A thimble is a small metal or plastic cap worn on the tip of your finger. When you sew, you push a needle through cloth, and the thimble stops the needle from pricking you. «The Dodo presents the thimble with "We beg your acceptance of this elegant thimble", and they all cheer.» Here it is a real object given as a prize.
explain: The second meaning is a tiny amount, as in "a thimbleful". If the sentence talks about pouring, drinking or a small amount, you need this meaning. To check, swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A thimble is a small metal or plastic cap worn on your finger to protect it when you sew.
fact: «Thimble» can also mean a tiny amount, as in «a thimbleful» of water.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Dodo takes her own thimble and gives it to Alice as a prize.
fact: Pupils who hand-sew in textiles or D and T lessons often wear a thimble to protect the finger that pushes the needle.
fact: "Hunt the thimble" is an old party game, so a thimble can also be a small object in a game.
exercise: «a thimble protects the pushing finger.» What is a thimble here? | A large cup for drinking tea | A type of cloth used for dresses | A needle kept in a pincushion | *A small metal or plastic cap worn on your finger when sewing
exercise: Mum poured a thimbleful of milk into the cup. How much milk is that? | A big bottle | A full glass | *Only a tiny amount | A whole jug
exercise: Find one word that means given in a formal way: «the Dodo takes her own thimble and solemnly presents it back to her» | *presents | takes | thimble | everyone
test: «all she has left is a thimble» in the story. What is a thimble here? | *A small cap worn on a finger to protect it | A tiny cup for tea | A dress | A kind of needle
test: Sam says, "Add a thimbleful of water." How much water is that? | A whole bucket | Half a bottle | *Only a tiny amount | A lot
exam: Which meaning fits «a thimbleful of milk»? | *A tiny amount | A small cap worn on a finger | A metal bucket | A sewing needle
